## Invariants on Enum Types

```xiom
pub type PaymentMethod = enum {
  CreditCard(number: Str, expiry: Str, cvv: Int),
  BankAccount(iban: Str, bic: Str),
  Cash,
}
  invariant: match this {
    PaymentMethod::CreditCard(n, e, c) => n.len() == 16 && c >= 100 && c <= 999,
    PaymentMethod::BankAccount(i, b) => i.len() >= 15 && b.len() >= 8,
    PaymentMethod::Cash => true,
  };
```

### Validating Optional Fields
```xiom
pub type UserProfile = enum {
  Anonymous,
  Registered(name: Str, email: Str, age: Int),
}
  invariant: match this {
    UserProfile::Registered(n, e, a) => n.len() > 0 && e.contains("@") && a >= 13,
    UserProfile::Anonymous => true,
  };
```

### Nested Invariants with Structs
```xiom
pub type Point = {
  x: Float64;
  y: Float64;
}

pub type Shape = enum {
  Circle(center: Point, radius: Float64),
  Rectangle(top_left: Point, bottom_right: Point),
}
  invariant: match this {
    Shape::Circle(_, r) => r > 0.0,
    Shape::Rectangle(tl, br) => tl.x <= br.x && tl.y <= br.y,
  };
```

### Invariant vs Constructor Validation
An invariant on an enum is checked EVERY time the value is created or modified — including through pattern matching reassignment. A constructor only checks at build time.

